22.1  Units and timers

TL;DR
  - systemd manages units: services (.service), timers (.timer), mounts,
    sockets, and targets, which group other units.
  - start and stop act now. enable and disable decide what starts at boot. They
    are separate.
  - A timer starts a service on a schedule: it is systemd's version of cron.

      systemctl status nginx
      sudo systemctl enable --now nginx   # start now, and at every boot
      systemctl list-timers

On your machine
  State     degraded: some units failed
  Failed    backup.service (Nightly backup)

Pitfalls
  - enable alone doesn't start the service now: add --now, or run start too.
  - After editing a unit file, run sudo systemctl daemon-reload, or systemd
    keeps using the old version.
  - mask is stronger than disable: a masked unit can't start at all, not even by
    hand or as a dependency.
  - systemctl --user manages your own units, separately from the system's, and
    without sudo.

Deep dive

  ▾ What is a target?

    A target is a named group of units that marks a stage: multi-user.target is
    a working system without a graphical login, graphical.target adds one. At
    boot, systemd starts everything the default target needs (see 20.1). Targets
    replaced the numbered runlevels of the older SysV init.

  ▾ Timers or cron?

    Timers log every run in the journal, can run a job missed while the machine
    was off (Persistent=true), and their service can be started by hand to test
    it. A timer runs the service of the same name:

        [Timer]
        OnCalendar=*-*-* 03:00:00
        Persistent=true

    cron stays simpler for a one-line job, and is still available.

Related
  Commands  systemctl
  Paths     /etc/systemd/system, /usr/lib/systemd/system
  See also  atfq 22.2 Why a service won't start
  See also  atfq 22.3 Unit files and overrides
  See also  atfq 20.1 From firmware to init
